; bfsodium KECCAK SPONGE 168 : absorb at rate 168 and squeeze as much as is asked
;
; HAND WRITTEN; FIPS 202 section 4's sponge over keccak/permute1600  at the rate
; SHAKE128 uses; It is keccak/sponge136 with one number changed and every
; distance that number reaches worked out again  which is the whole cost of a
; rate in this library and the reason a rate cannot be a parameter;
;
; INTERFACE entry=352 exit=1056 footprint=0:1415
; IO  in:  pad{1}  mlen{2} LE  olen{2} LE  plen{2} LE  pbuf{336}  slen{2} LE  sbuf{8}
;          then mlen bytes from the wire
;     out: olen bytes  as they come off the state
;
; THE RATE CANNOT BE A PARAMETER BUT THE PADDING BYTE CAN; A rate decides how
; many lanes the absorb touches and how far every one of its journeys runs  and
; a journey of a computed length needs an index  which this library does not
; have; A padding byte is one cell of data; So there is one sponge file per rate
; and each hands the padding byte it was given to the block: 31 for SHAKE128 and
; later 4 for cSHAKE128 and KMAC128 at the same rate;
;
; TAPE MAP  (home @0)
;   @0x000:0x0c7  S{200}  the sponge state; it starts at NOUGHT  which is why
;                         the five bytes read below are moved up out of it
;   @0x0c8        save    keccak/rotstate borrows this one cell and hands it
;                         back at nought  so it costs the permutation nothing
;                         and needs no room of its own
;   @0x0c8:0x330  keccak/permute1600's frame  pasted at this file's own zero
;   @0x338:0x3df  BB{168} the block being filled; one RATE of the sponge
;   @0x3e8:0x40d  the exclusive or frame; xor64 pasted at @0x3e8
;   @0x410        mlo     message bytes still to come  low byte
;   @0x411        mhi     and high byte
;   @0x412        msgleft one while either of those is not nought
;   @0x413        pad     one until the padding byte has been placed
;   @0x414        more    one while another block is wanted
;   @0x415        ic      the hundred and sixty eight bytes of a block  and
;                         later the two hundred cells of one turn of the state
;   @0x416:0x41b  t0 t1 e0 t2 t3 e1  the cells the tests are made through
;   @0x41c        last    one if the padding byte was placed in THIS block
;   @0x41d        olo     output bytes still owed  low byte
;   @0x41e        ohi     and high byte
;   @0x41f        padb    the padding byte  as it came in on the wire
;   @0x420        oleft   one while either olo or ohi is not nought
;   @0x421        kc      bytes still allowed out of the rate in hand
;   @0x422        sleft   one while the suffix still has a byte to give
;   @0x423        selse   one until the suffix arm has fired  so the
;                         padding does not fire on the same byte
;   @0x424:0x425  st0 st1 the cells the suffix tests are made through
;   @0x426:0x427  slen{2} how much suffix the sponge takes from the tape
;   @0x428:0x429  plen{2} and how much prefix; both counts are SPENT
;   @0x42a:0x42d  the prefix's own else arm  test cells and flag
;   @0x430:0x57f  pbuf{336} the prefix buffer  two whole rates of it
;   @0x580:0x587  sbuf{8} the suffix buffer; right_encode of a length is
;                         at most five bytes  and eight is the round
;                         number above it
;
; THIS MAP IS SPONGE136's SHIFTED BY EXACTLY 32 above the block  and that is not
; a coincidence but the thing that made this file cheap: the block starts at
; 0x338 in both  so it ENDS 32 cells higher here  and the flag cluster was put
; 32 cells higher to match; Every walk between the TOP of the block and a flag
; cell therefore has the SAME distance in both files; Only the two walks that
; touch the BOTTOM of the block changed  and the twenty one lane journeys;
;
; THE RATE IS A HUNDRED AND SIXTY EIGHT and that is exactly TWENTY ONE LANES  so
; absorbing a block is twenty one entries of xor64 rather than a hundred and
; sixty eight byte exclusive ors; Every rate in the family divides by eight the
; same way: 144 for SHA3_224 is eighteen lanes  136 for SHA3_256 and SHAKE256 is
; seventeen  and 72 for SHA3_512 is nine;
;
; AND EVERY OPERAND IS MOVED  never copied: the state lane is replaced by the
; answer and the block is spent  so nothing needs handing back;
;
; THE BLOCK IS FILLED BY A CONVEYOR  because there is no index: a byte always
; enters at the TOP of the block and the block slides down one before the next
; one arrives; So the byte that enters first has slid a hundred and sixty seven
; times when the block is full and comes to rest at the bottom  which is where
; it belongs;
;
; AND THE BLOCK'S BYTES COME FROM FOUR PLACES IN ORDER  which is the one
; thing to know before reading the byte source below: the TAPE PREFIX  then
; the WIRE  then the TAPE SUFFIX  then the padding; cSHAKE and KMAC put whole
; bytepad blocks in front of the message and KMAC puts right_encode of the
; output length behind it  and neither is on the wire; Each tape arm is a
; SECOND CONVEYOR feeding the first  so the block machinery below is the same
; in all four cases and only the source of the next byte differs;
;
; NOTHING IN THE BLOCK ACCOUNTING KNOWS ABOUT ANY OF THEM  and that is why a
; suffix may spill into a block of its own: whether another block is wanted is
; decided by pad  which stays set until the padding byte is placed  and the
; padding waits behind the suffix;
;
; THE PADDING IS FIPS 202's  and the conveyor makes it almost free: when the
; message runs out the next byte to enter is the padding byte  and after that
; nothing enters at all  which leaves noughts behind the conveyor exactly as the
; rule wants; The last byte of the LAST block then gains 128; That byte is either
; nought or the padding byte itself  and neither 31 nor 4 carries when 128 is
; added  so the case where the message is a whole number of blocks needs no
; special arm: the padding simply gets a block of its own;
;
; ON THE LAST BLOCK AND NOT ON EVERY BLOCK; The block in which the padding byte
; was placed is the last block by definition  so placing it sets a flag and the
; 128 is added under that flag; The first version of the rate 136 sponge did it
; on every block and only failed once a message was long enough to need two;
;
; AND THE SQUEEZE IS A LOOP  because SHAKE's output may be longer than a rate:
; the state is stirred again between one rate and the next; Within a rate the
; byte to go out is always the BOTTOM cell of the state  because
; keccak/rotstate turns the state over one cell at a time; Two hundred turns put
; it back exactly as the permutation left it  so a rate is squeezed by turning
; the state over all two hundred of its cells and sending a byte out on the first
; hundred and sixty eight of those turns  or on as many as are still owed;
;
; AND THE STIR COMES AFTER THE RATE AND NOT BEFORE IT  under a test of whether
; anything is still owed  so a caller that asks for a rate or less pays for the
; permutation the absorb already did and not one more;
;
; A BLOCK IS ABOUT TWO AND A HALF BILLION INSTRUCTIONS  nearly all of it the
; permutation at two thousand four hundred and eighty four million; The conveyor
; that fills a block costs about seven million and the absorb thirty  and one
; whole turn of the state to squeeze a rate is thirty six million;
;
; A RATE OF 168 IS THE CHEAPER SPONGE PER BYTE and that is worth knowing: a
; block and a rate cost what they cost here regardless of the rate  so absorbing
; a message takes a permutation every 168 bytes rather than every 136  and
; squeezing gives 168 bytes per permutation rather than 136; SHAKE128 is about a
; quarter cheaper per byte than SHAKE256 for that reason alone;
;
; The five bytes of the head are read into the bottom of the STATE and moved up
; out of it  because a read prologue may hold nothing but commas and steps and
; the state must start at nought; Every distance below was worked out from the
; map;

; read the padding byte  the message length  the output length  the prefix
; length  the prefix buffer  the suffix length and the suffix buffer
